      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Fractures of the zygomatic arch extended to the glenoid fossa are rare and seldom reported."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "OBSERVATION",
            "content": "A 26-year-old man with a slightly dislocated fracture of the zygoma associated with trismus, and localized pain in the right Temporo-Mandibular Joint (TMJ) on palpation, but without any condylar fracture. The craniofacial CT scan revealed a fracture of the zygoma extended to the glenoid fossa. The treatment was an intraoral reduction of the zygoma and screw fixation of the glenoid fossa lateral region."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "The risk of delayed healing by mobilization of the bone fragments was opposed to the risk of TMJ ankylosis due to a prolonged immobilization of the joint. Osteosynthesis of the fracture was finally chosen."
          }
        ]
      },
